  "summary": "The picture you stared at for three minutes: borders, logos and hope.",
  "description": "On the Commodore 64, the loading screen was half the product: while cassette tape hissed through loader bars, players stared at title pictures squeezed into the C64's attribute-grid — multicolor char-cell art with forced colors per 8x8 cell — produced by graphics houses like The Designers Republic precursors, Bob Wakelin and Oliver Frey for Zzap!64-adjacent studios. Ocean, Imagine and Hewson commissioned painted-to-pixel conversions that were often better than the games; cracktros later gave the same slots to demos. The grid-constrained look (two colors per cell, charset glyphs as texture) is a distinctive pixel-art dialect still imitated in modern indie covers.",
  "culturalContext": "C64 loading screens made waiting into looking: tape-loader art was often better than the games, the attribute-grid look is a distinctive pixel-art dialect, and cracktros plus preservation archives (C64.com, Zzap!64 scans) treat loading screens as the UK-Europe software industry's folk gallery.",
